# A to Z style guide

This guide covers general writing. For Home Assistant words, see [Technical A to Z](technical-a-to-z.html). Each entry gives the rule, then an example.

## A

### Abbreviations and acronyms

<span id="wg-024"></span>Write the full term the first time, with the abbreviation in brackets. Use the abbreviation after that. Do not expand an abbreviation everyone knows, such as PDF or USB. Keep the unit symbol next to the number.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| The PIR fired. | The passive infrared (PIR) sensor reported movement. |
| The PDF (portable document format) is ready. | The PDF is ready. |

If an abbreviation only matters in diagnostics, put it in a details section, not in a button label.

### Ampersand

Use “and”, not “&”.

## B

### Bold

Use bold only for an interface element in an instruction. Do not use it to add emphasis. The one exception is a safety warning, where a single critical word may be bold. See [Write a warning](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-042).

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Do **not** unplug the hub. | Do not unplug the hub. |
| Select Save. | Select **Save**. |

### Bullet points

See [Text formatting](../formatting-content/text-formatting.html).

## C

### Capital letters and punctuation

<span id="wg-023"></span>Use sentence case for headings, labels and buttons, even in page titles. Use capitals only for the first word, names and product names. Do not write whole sentences in capital letters. A full sentence ends in a full stop. A short label or heading does not.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Save Schedule | Save schedule |
| SAVE SCHEDULE | Save schedule |
| Turn Off All Lights. | Turn off all lights |
| Heating Is On | Heating is on. |

### Click

Use “select”, not “click”. Not everyone clicks.

### Contractions

In warnings, conditions and short instructions, write “cannot” and “do not”, not “can’t” and “don’t”. Elsewhere “you’ll” and “it’s” are fine. Pick one form for each word and keep it on one screen. Avoid “should’ve” and “would’ve”. See [Write important conditions in full](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-014).

## D

### Dates

Write the day, then the month in full, then the year: 2 June 2026. Do not use “2nd”, a comma between month and year, or numbers only (06/02/26). Use “to” in a range, not a dash: 2 June to 9 June.

## E

### E.g., i.e. and etc

Use “for example”, “such as” or “including”. Avoid “etc”. Never use “etc” after “for example”.

### Email

One word, lower case. Write “email address”, not “email”, when you mean the address.

## H

### Headings

Use sentence case. Start with a verb where you can: “Pair a motion sensor”. Do not use a question or “Introduction”.

## I

### Italics

Do not use italics. Use ‘single quotation marks’ for a document or scheme name.

## M

### Measurements

Use numerals and no space before the unit: 3,500kg, 21°C, 4.62kWh. Use Celsius.

## N

### Numbers

Write numbers as numerals, including 1 to 9: “3 lights are on”. Write “one” only inside a phrase such as “one of the lights”. Use a comma in thousands: 1,200.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Three of four lights are on. | 3 of 4 lights are on. |
| 1200 readings | 1,200 readings |

## P

### Please

Do not say “please” or “please note”. Say what to do.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Please note that the hub will restart. | The hub will restart. |

## T

### Times

Use the household’s clock setting and keep to it on one screen. On a 12-hour clock write 5:30pm. On a 24-hour clock write 17:30. Never write 1730hrs. Use “to” in a range: 10am to 11am. Write “midnight” and “midday”, not 00:00, 12pm or “noon”. Midnight is the first minute of the day. For a deadline, use 11:59pm and the date.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| 10-11am | 10am to 11am |
| 12 noon | midday |
| Off at 00:00 | Off at midnight, the start of Tuesday 14 June |
| 5:30pm in the text, 17:30 in the table | 5:30pm in both, or 17:30 in both |

## W

### Wifi

Write “wifi” in lower case, with no hyphen, unless you are quoting a native Home Assistant label that spells it differently.
